{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.substrate_satisfies_hypothesis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.substrate_satisfies_hypothesis","kind":"theorem","line":252,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L252-L256","doc":"**Non-vacuity at the substrate anchor.** `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` satisfies the\nhypothesis with room: `q³ ≈ 1.3676` against `q² + 1 ≈ 2.2321`. Stated on a\nrational bracket so it is checkable by `norm_num`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.reciprocal_ratio_strictMono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.reciprocal_ratio_strictMono","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L241-L250","doc":"**The consequence, as an ordering statement.** With `0 < b < a` (the corpus's\nforced ordering `μ₋ < μ₊`), a common factor keeps the symmetric channel the\nlarger one; only a reciprocal grading can lift the ratio further. Stated as the\nstrict monotonicity that makes the threshold in the probe well-defined. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.reciprocal_factors_scale_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.reciprocal_factors_scale_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":230,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L230-L239","doc":"**A reciprocal (T-odd) grading does move the ratio.** If the two channels\nreceive `t` and `t⁻¹`, the ratio is multiplied by `t²` — strictly monotone in `t`,\nso the channels can be reordered.\n\nThis is the ribbon twist: `θ_λ = q^{c(λ)}` with `c(λ') = -c(λ)`\n(`QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneModularTwist`), so at `n = 2` the symmetric block gets\n`q^{f}` and the antisymmetric block `q^{-f}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.common_factor_cancels_in_ratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.common_factor_cancels_in_ratio","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L219-L228","doc":"**A T-blind grading cannot discriminate the channels.** If both channels are\nscaled by the same factor `c ≠ 0`, their ratio is unchanged — so no common\ngrading can change which channel is closer to the unbinding threshold.\n\nThis is the writhe: the normalisation `(1-h)^(e/2)` depends on the exponent sum,\nwhich is a property of the *word*, not of the Wedderburn block, so both channels\nreceive it identically and it cancels here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.unbinding_needs_large_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.unbinding_needs_large_q","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L201-L211","doc":"**Contrapositive: unbinding needs a large `q`.** If some admissible single\ncrossing fails to bind, then `q² + 1 ≤ q³` — i.e. `q` is at least the real root\nof `q³ = q² + 1` (`≈ 1.46557`). The substrate anchor `q₀ ≈ 1.10998` is 24 % below\nthat, so this is not a near-miss. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.t3_grading_cannot_unbind_pp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.t3_grading_cannot_unbind_pp","kind":"theorem","line":180,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L180-L199","doc":"**The `T₃` grading provably cannot unbind the `pp` channel.**\n\nThe corpus grades the strong-isospin interface by crossing sign — `σ` (positive\nwrithe) for `pp`, `σ⁻¹` (negative) for `nn` — i.e. by `T₃`\n(`QOU.ParticleInteractions.WeakIsospinT3Grading`). A `T₃` grading therefore hands\n`pp` a **non-negative** writhe exponent, and by `nonneg_writhe_cannot_unbind` any\nnon-negative exponent keeps the channel bound.\n\nSo the failure is not that the framework picked the wrong magnitude for `pp`: it\npicked a sign that makes unbinding **impossible**, whatever the magnitude. Nature\nhas `a^N_pp = −17.3 fm` — unbound.\n\nThe `nn` side is not excluded by this (its `T₃` writhe is negative, the helpful\ndirection) — but it is quantitatively far off: unbinding `nn` needs `e ≤ −7.74`\nat the substrate anchor against the `T₃` assignment's `e = −1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.nonneg_writhe_cannot_unbind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.nonneg_writhe_cannot_unbind","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L163-L178","doc":"**A non-negative writhe exponent can never unbind.** The writhe grading sends\n`μ ↦ μ · (1-h)^(e/2)` with `0 < 1-h < 1`. For `e ≥ 0` that factor is `≤ 1`, so a\nchannel already in `(0,1)` stays there.\n\nThis is the general form of `writhe_normalisation_binds_harder`, stated on the\nreal exponent so it applies to a whole grading rather than one factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.writhe_normalisation_binds_harder","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.writhe_normalisation_binds_harder","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L150-L161","doc":"**The writhe normalisation cannot rescue the sign.** It multiplies `μ` by\n`(1-h)^(e/2)`, a factor in `(0,1)` when `0 < h < 1` and the exponent is positive;\na smaller `μ` binds *harder*. Stated on the factor directly so it applies to any\npositive grading exponent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.single_crossing_binds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.single_crossing_binds","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L141-L148","doc":"**Every admissible single-crossing interface BINDS.** With `B = -α·log μ` and\n`α > 0`, `μ < 1` gives `B > 0`. No choice of `d` avoids it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.interface_mem_Ioo","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.interface_mem_Ioo","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L130-L139","doc":"**The admissible interface family lies in `(0, 1)`.** `d` runs over\n`[-h, 0]`: `d = 0` is `σ`, `d = -h` is `σ⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.h_lt_markovZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.h_lt_markovZ","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L122-L128","doc":"**`h < z` for every `q > 0`, unconditionally**, by the exact gap above. This\nkeeps the whole admissible family strictly positive, so `log μ` is defined on all\nof it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovZ_sub_heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovZ_sub_heckeH","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L107-L120","doc":"**The gap between `z` and `h` is exactly `1/(q(q²+1))`.** The `q⁴` terms\ncancel identically:\n\n    z - h = q³/(q²+1) - (q²-1)/q = [q⁴ - (q⁴ - 1)] / [q(q²+1)] = 1/[q(q²+1)].\n\nSo the bottom of the admissible family sits a computable distance above `0`, for\nevery `q > 0` — there is no `q` at which the family degenerates. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovZ_lt_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovZ_lt_one","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L98-L105","doc":"**`z < 1` is exactly `q³ < q² + 1`.** This is the whole mechanism: the\ninterface family is capped by `z`, so this inequality decides whether any single\ncrossing can fail to bind. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovZ_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovZ_pos","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L94-L96","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.heckeH","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.heckeH","kind":"def","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L91-L92","doc":"The Hecke parameter `h = q - q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.markovZ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.markovZ","kind":"def","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/SingleCrossingAlwaysBinds.lean#L88-L89","doc":"The Markov parameter of convention D. ","signatureChunk":0}]}